        "abstract_source_sha256": "5d1145a052135d34997614be42142f9b63ef76730ca9992e045d52548747dd7e",
        "abstract_tex": "Costabel's 2022 Oberwolfach report asks for a precise description and proof of the observed approximation of nonreal eigenvalues by the discrete dipole approximation away from a real lattice interval. We give an independent proof for bounded particles whose center-selected cubic voxels converge in volume. This includes every bounded Lipschitz particle, with arbitrary grid translations. At each fixed complex frequency, the actual zero-diagonal matrices have no spectral pollution outside the exact static lattice interval; isolated eigenvalues are approximated with algebraic multiplicity and norm-convergent embedded Riesz projections. The proof combines the established static lattice bound of Costabel--Dauge--Nedaiasl with strong static consistency, Hilbert--Schmidt convergence of the sampled dynamic correction and classical spectral approximation. A separate compact, positive-volume, empty-interior support has a nonreal continuum eigenvalue at frequency $10^{-4}$ while every matrix on the grids $n^{-1}\\Z^3$ is $3\\times3$ zero. This example clarifies the geometric hypothesis. The classical ingredients and previously announced dynamic work are explicitly credited; first priority is not certified. AI tools were used extensively, and no external human peer review is asserted.",
